I'm always for tax cuts,
you cut taxes on business,

00:32:54.572 --> 00:32:55.773 align:start size:84% position:16%
now people are gonna
have more money.

00:32:55.773 --> 00:32:57.208
You cut personal income tax,

00:32:57.208 --> 00:32:58.443
people are gonna
have more money.

00:32:58.443 --> 00:33:00.111 align:start size:84% position:16%
I would much rather
people decide

00:33:00.111 --> 00:33:02.780 align:start size:94% position:6%
what to do with their
money than the government,

00:33:02.780 --> 00:33:05.383
but yet a huge percentage
of people seem to think,

00:33:05.383 --> 00:33:08.319
"Oh, we just have to keep
throwing money at things.

00:33:08.319 --> 00:33:09.887 align:start size:84% position:16%
"No matter what
the evidence says."

00:33:09.887 --> 00:33:12.857
Is that like just one
of the ongoing battles

00:33:12.857 --> 00:33:15.293
that you have to have
sort of intellectually.

00:33:15.293 --> 00:33:19.063 align:start position:0%
- Part of it is part of member,
some of it is self-serving.

00:33:19.063 --> 00:33:24.068
I mean, if you're a racial
studies graduate from Harvard

00:33:25.236 --> 00:33:26.738
and you have this great
new idea for a program

00:33:26.738 --> 00:33:27.939
where the federal government

00:33:27.939 --> 00:33:30.808 align:start size:84% position:16%
will hire 600 racial
studies experts,

00:33:30.808 --> 00:33:33.344
to brainwash the rest of us.

00:33:33.344 --> 00:33:34.445
I mean, you just figured out

00:33:34.445 --> 00:33:36.814
a whole new career for yourself.

00:33:36.814 --> 00:33:38.583
If you're the teacher's union,

00:33:38.583 --> 00:33:39.984
you'd love to have more money.

00:33:39.984 --> 00:33:41.853 align:start size:97% position:3%
I mean, that's understandable
the same thing's true

00:33:41.853 --> 00:33:43.654 align:start size:81% position:19%
for the police
union, but I mean.

00:33:43.654 --> 00:33:48.226 align:start size:97% position:3%
So there's a certain amount
of self-aggrandizement.

00:33:48.226 --> 00:33:49.727
There's a certain look,

00:33:49.727 --> 00:33:53.698
there's a fair amount of not
thinking about this stuff,

00:33:53.698 --> 00:33:55.833
for the practical reason
that in a free society,

00:33:55.833 --> 00:33:57.869
most of the time, you
shouldn't have to.

00:34:00.071 --> 00:34:02.840
And our education system right
now is so totally screwed up.

00:34:02.840 --> 00:34:05.843 align:start size:97% position:3%
And as Reagan once said,
"It's now what they don't know

00:34:05.843 --> 00:34:08.179
"that's scary, it's what
they know that isn't true."

00:34:08.179 --> 00:34:10.214 align:start size:88% position:13%
Let me say one more
thing about this stuff.

00:34:10.214 --> 00:34:12.984
One of the great lessons
I learned was from a book

00:34:14.085 --> 00:34:16.988 align:start size:88% position:13%
by Tony Schwartz called
the election game

00:34:16.988 --> 00:34:17.822
and then how to win.

00:34:17.822 --> 00:34:18.656
And I'm sorry.

00:34:19.824 --> 00:34:21.092
I won't get the name,
the title right now,

00:34:21.092 --> 00:34:22.693
I'm just gonna give
you the wrong title.

00:34:22.693 --> 00:34:25.596
Anyway, Tony Schwartz was
a great radio consultant

00:34:25.596 --> 00:34:26.798
who then went into politics.

00:34:26.798 --> 00:34:29.033
He did a lots of radio ads

00:34:29.033 --> 00:34:32.637
and he was very good at
understanding the idea

00:34:32.637 --> 00:34:35.706
that was called "The
Responsive Chord."

00:34:35.706 --> 00:34:37.942
His theory was, he wanted
to talk about something

00:34:37.942 --> 00:34:40.144
that was already in your brain.

00:34:40.144 --> 00:34:43.047 align:start size:88% position:13%
So all he's doing is
triggering the response.

00:34:43.047 --> 00:34:46.617 align:start size:97% position:3%
He's not filling it up, but
he had a very good sound rule.

00:34:46.617 --> 00:34:48.352
he said,

00:34:48.352 --> 00:34:52.457
"Never underestimate
the intelligence of
the American people

00:34:53.891 --> 00:34:55.393 align:start size:97% position:3%
"nor overestimate the amount
of information they have.

00:34:57.095 --> 00:34:59.130 align:start size:94% position:6%
"Very often they won't
have the right information

00:34:59.130 --> 00:35:01.432
"and so they'll look
like they don't know,

00:35:01.432 --> 00:35:04.769 align:start position:0%
"but if you'll make sure to
get them the right information,

00:35:04.769 --> 00:35:07.371
"they will then figure
out the right solution."

00:35:07.371 --> 00:35:10.675 align:start size:97% position:3%
And I found in my career,
that was almost always true.

00:35:10.675 --> 00:35:12.376
And I'm trying to convince,

00:35:12.376 --> 00:35:14.278 align:start size:84% position:16%
frankly the
Republicans this year,

00:35:14.278 --> 00:35:16.614 align:start size:84% position:16%
that if they'll calmly
and methodically

00:35:16.614 --> 00:35:19.484
run a very fact-based campaign,

00:35:19.484 --> 00:35:21.085 align:start size:94% position:6%
that they'll be shocked
at how well they'll do it.

00:35:21.085 --> 00:35:21.919
For example,

00:35:23.354 --> 00:35:27.391
in a Pew poll recently, three
out of four Americans oppose

00:35:28.526 --> 00:35:29.827
defunding the police.

00:35:31.262 --> 00:35:35.766 align:start size:97% position:3%
Now, and frankly of the 25%
who are in favor of defunding,

00:35:35.766 --> 00:35:38.603 align:start size:84% position:16%
about half of them
don't wanna very much.

00:35:38.603 --> 00:35:41.472 align:start size:84% position:16%
So the really hardcore
left wing radicals

00:35:41.472 --> 00:35:43.741 align:start size:84% position:16%
are about 12 or
13% of the country.

00:35:44.809 --> 00:35:46.077
Well, I mean,

00:35:46.077 --> 00:35:48.212
Republicans ought to be
able to win that fight.

00:35:48.212 --> 00:35:49.580
You ought to be able to beat

00:35:49.580 --> 00:35:52.116 align:start position:0%
a whole bunch of House
Democrats who are running around

00:35:52.116 --> 00:35:55.386
and then Senate Democrats,
and frankly since Biden,

00:35:55.386 --> 00:35:57.321
no Biden is in this wishy washy.

00:35:57.321 --> 00:35:59.957
"Well, I'm really not
for totally defunding

00:35:59.957 --> 00:36:01.759 align:start size:84% position:16%
"but I'm for a little
bit defunding."

00:36:02.727 --> 00:36:04.195
That ain't gonna cut it not.

00:36:04.195 --> 00:36:08.032 align:start size:88% position:13%
Now when you say to him,
"So two weekends ago,

00:36:08.032 --> 00:36:11.035
"there were 152 Americans shot

00:36:11.035 --> 00:36:14.038 align:start size:84% position:16%
"in New York and
Chicago combined, 152.

00:36:15.239 --> 00:36:17.275 align:start size:81% position:19%
"So Mr. Biden,
what's your answer

00:36:17.275 --> 00:36:19.810
"to 152 Americans being shot?"

00:36:19.810 --> 00:36:21.712
And the truth is
he has no answer.

00:36:23.114 --> 00:36:26.751 align:start position:0%
- Are you shocked then that
they ended up going with Biden?

00:36:26.751 --> 00:36:28.753
I mean, he's been
around forever.

00:36:28.753 --> 00:36:30.254
I know you're no fan
of Hillary Clinton,

00:36:30.254 --> 00:36:32.723
but at least there was some base

00:36:32.723 --> 00:36:35.293
that was seriously
excited about her,

00:36:35.293 --> 00:36:37.061
that that's undeniable,

00:36:37.061 --> 00:36:41.065 align:start size:97% position:3%
but I don't see anyone
that's excited about Biden.

00:36:41.065 --> 00:36:43.000
And if anything,

00:36:43.000 --> 00:36:45.169 align:start size:88% position:13%
he's obviously having
some cognitive problems,

00:36:45.169 --> 00:36:48.039
and the media seems to
be running cover for it.

00:36:48.039 --> 00:36:51.209
And I don't mean that to be
glib or funny or anything.

00:36:51.209 --> 00:36:52.577
I mean it's sad in a way,

00:36:52.577 --> 00:36:55.680 align:start size:97% position:3%
but that there's no
excitement behind this thing.

00:36:55.680 --> 00:36:59.183 align:start position:0%
Do you think they're gonna
regret that this was the choice?

00:36:59.183 --> 00:37:01.118 align:start size:88% position:13%
- Well, you actually got
me to think something

00:37:01.118 --> 00:37:02.253
I hadn't thought about.

00:37:03.654 --> 00:37:08.292 align:start size:94% position:6%
There's a fascinating,
almost 180 degree opposite

00:37:09.460 --> 00:37:13.564
between the Republican
selection process of 16,

00:37:13.564 --> 00:37:16.000
and the Biden process.

00:37:17.168 --> 00:37:19.804 align:start size:84% position:16%
In 16, Trump kept
knocking people out.

00:37:20.972 --> 00:37:23.274
And then he started,
I think with Jeb Bush.

00:37:23.274 --> 00:37:25.710 align:start size:88% position:13%
And he just methodically
went after people

00:37:25.710 --> 00:37:28.012
and just kept knocking them out.

00:37:28.012 --> 00:37:29.547
Biden, passively,

00:37:29.547 --> 00:37:31.682
allowed everybody else
to shoot themselves.

00:37:33.084 --> 00:37:35.286 align:start size:94% position:6%
So they would, I mean,
everybody got a shot at it

00:37:35.286 --> 00:37:37.288 align:start size:88% position:13%
and everybody had
their moment in the sun

00:37:37.288 --> 00:37:38.823
and then they fail.

00:37:38.823 --> 00:37:41.325
And so people say, "Well,
not him or what not her."

00:37:41.325 --> 00:37:44.895
And Biden ultimately was I
think relying on two things

00:37:44.895 --> 00:37:48.199
he started the dance
with very high name ID.

00:37:49.367 --> 00:37:51.602
I think only Sanders
had equal name ID.

00:37:51.602 --> 00:37:52.436
And second,

00:37:54.672 --> 00:37:59.410 align:start size:97% position:3%
he had spent eight years
standing next to Barack Obama.

00:38:00.711 --> 00:38:02.146
And in South Carolina,

00:38:02.146 --> 00:38:06.150 align:start position:0%
if you can carry the black
vote in the Democratic primary,

00:38:06.150 --> 00:38:07.551
you will win the primary.

00:38:08.953 --> 00:38:13.624 align:start size:94% position:6%
And his entire reason
for being, was to set up,

00:38:15.359 --> 00:38:16.527
getting to South Carolina.

00:38:16.527 --> 00:38:19.864 align:start size:88% position:13%
So as long as he didn't
knock himself out,

00:38:19.864 --> 00:38:23.067
none of the rest of them had
the ability to knock him out.

00:38:23.067 --> 00:38:25.236
And he just said to
be standing there

00:38:25.236 --> 00:38:27.371 align:start size:97% position:3%
and then they did something
very smart, which is,

00:38:27.371 --> 00:38:30.174
I'm sure they work
behind the scenes

00:38:30.174 --> 00:38:32.710 align:start position:0%
and they got three or four
people to decide to endorse him

00:38:32.710 --> 00:38:34.679
as soon as he won
South Carolina.

00:38:34.679 --> 00:38:37.181
So you suddenly had this
momentum of excitement

00:38:37.181 --> 00:38:38.983
that carries into Super Tuesday.

00:38:40.851 --> 00:38:42.320
(Newt coughs)

00:38:42.320 --> 00:38:44.522 align:start position:0%
And think about it by Super
Tuesday you're down to Sanders,

00:38:45.723 --> 00:38:48.959
who however far to
the left Biden is

00:38:48.959 --> 00:38:51.896
Sanders will always be 20
feet further to the left.

00:38:51.896 --> 00:38:54.899 align:start size:97% position:3%
The Biden moves left, Sanders
move even further left.

00:38:54.899 --> 00:38:56.600
So in the end Sanders,

00:38:56.600 --> 00:38:59.537
his base was probably
40 or 45% of the party.

00:39:01.439 --> 00:39:03.040 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Do you think we
have to do anything?

00:39:03.040 --> 00:39:05.676
I know you're not a Democrat
obviously, but on either side,

00:39:05.676 --> 00:39:07.712
as far as the primary process,

00:39:07.712 --> 00:39:09.046
they run primaries
very differently.

00:39:09.046 --> 00:39:10.848
We saw the disaster in Iowa.

00:39:10.848 --> 00:39:12.650 align:start size:94% position:6%
Do you think we just have
to continue with this

00:39:12.650 --> 00:39:14.151 align:start size:84% position:16%
or is there anything
in modern times

00:39:14.151 --> 00:39:16.721
that we should do to
change some of this?

00:39:16.721 --> 00:39:18.122
- I mean, look,

00:39:18.122 --> 00:39:20.391 align:start size:97% position:3%
I think the Democratic
Party's incompetence in Iowa,

00:39:20.391 --> 00:39:23.728
is just part of how badly
structured their party is.

00:39:26.163 --> 00:39:29.400 align:start size:97% position:3%
I mean, we had a very similar
problem by the way in 12,

00:39:29.400 --> 00:39:32.837 align:start size:94% position:6%
because the truth was that
Rick Santorum won Iowa.

00:39:32.837 --> 00:39:37.842
The party chairman at that
time called it for Romney.

00:39:39.210 --> 00:39:41.479 align:start size:94% position:6%
It took six or seven
days for it to be obvious

00:39:41.479 --> 00:39:43.013
that it was Santorum.

00:39:43.013 --> 00:39:46.951
So Santorum lost all of the
advantage he would have had

00:39:46.951 --> 00:39:48.652
going into New Hampshire.

00:39:48.652 --> 00:39:50.688
Romney had a false advantage.

00:39:51.822 --> 00:39:54.992
And the state chairman,
I think was in fact,

00:39:56.093 --> 00:39:58.028 align:start size:84% position:16%
economically leaning
towards Romney

00:39:58.028 --> 00:39:59.029
in ways that were totally in a,

00:39:59.029 --> 00:40:00.297
he had to resign.

00:40:01.665 --> 00:40:04.135 align:start size:88% position:13%
So I mean we've had
some of our own problems

00:40:04.135 --> 00:40:05.336
with this stuff,

00:40:05.336 --> 00:40:06.871
but I think the primary
process at one level

00:40:06.871 --> 00:40:10.274
was really a good thing
because it does allow you

00:40:12.009 --> 00:40:13.677
to go out and meet people.

00:40:13.677 --> 00:40:17.915
Now, the challenge is gonna be,

00:40:17.915 --> 00:40:22.253 align:start position:0%
as we move into this age of
social media on the giant scale,

00:40:23.421 --> 00:40:26.824 align:start size:88% position:13%
whether or not it makes
sense just because,

00:40:26.824 --> 00:40:29.794
we've been in the middle of a
full blown presidential race,

00:40:29.794 --> 00:40:32.129
I would guess for a year now.

00:40:34.532 --> 00:40:35.366
And,

00:40:37.601 --> 00:40:38.769
in that sense,

00:40:40.171 --> 00:40:42.673 align:start position:0%
it's not clear to me how things
will evolve in the future.

00:40:42.673 --> 00:40:45.476 align:start size:94% position:6% line:0%
Well, we also had absolute
proof for example,

00:40:45.476 --> 00:40:50.448 align:start size:88% position:13% line:0%
that the Bloomberg spent
a couple $100 million

00:40:51.549 --> 00:40:53.117
and got nowhere,

00:40:53.117 --> 00:40:56.086 align:start size:97% position:3%
because in the end when you
watched him on a debate stage,

00:40:56.086 --> 00:40:58.689
you just knew this
guy was impossible.

00:40:58.689 --> 00:41:02.927
So, it's business, how
it shapes things out.

00:41:03.828 --> 00:41:04.762
- Yeah.

00:41:04.762 --> 00:41:05.796
When you were watching Bloomberg

00:41:05.796 --> 00:41:07.064
in those two or three debates,

00:41:07.064 --> 00:41:08.666
I mean, as a guy,
that was a great,

00:41:08.666 --> 00:41:10.701
and are, you are
a great debater.

00:41:10.701 --> 00:41:12.336 align:start size:84% position:16%
I mean, you knew how
to use the crowd,

00:41:12.336 --> 00:41:14.004
you knew how to use humor,

00:41:14.004 --> 00:41:17.041 align:start size:88% position:13%
you knew how to directly
go after opponent.

00:41:17.041 --> 00:41:18.342
You must've been sort of painful

00:41:18.342 --> 00:41:19.944
for you to watch
some of these guys

00:41:19.944 --> 00:41:21.345
that just have no
idea how to debate.

00:41:21.345 --> 00:41:23.247
Is it painful or is it shocking,

00:41:23.247 --> 00:41:26.016 align:start size:94% position:6%
or do you feel good
about yourself out there?

00:41:26.016 --> 00:41:26.851
- No, it's fun.

00:41:27.918 --> 00:41:29.186
(Rubin laughs)

00:41:29.186 --> 00:41:31.388
- I mean, look, as long
as it's the other side,

00:41:31.388 --> 00:41:33.224
I'm happy for them
to be incompetent.

00:41:35.192 --> 00:41:37.394 align:start size:88% position:13%
I don't feel like I
have an obligation to...

00:41:37.394 --> 00:41:40.130 align:start size:88% position:13%
This is not like an
ice skating competition

00:41:40.130 --> 00:41:42.366 align:start size:88% position:13%
where I'm gonna give
them a nine or an eight.

00:41:43.267 --> 00:41:44.969
I watched some of it.

00:41:44.969 --> 00:41:49.139 align:start size:97% position:3%
I don't actually watch
probably as much as I should

00:41:49.139 --> 00:41:50.641
just 'cause I think it's boring

00:41:50.641 --> 00:41:53.611
and I can only take so much
of it, but I'll tell ya.

00:41:53.611 --> 00:41:58.315
And 16 and late 15,

00:41:58.315 --> 00:42:00.551 align:start size:94% position:6%
I guess it really started
in the summer of 15.

00:42:03.053 --> 00:42:04.955
I remember the first Fox debate,

00:42:04.955 --> 00:42:09.026 align:start size:94% position:6%
which was the first
debate of the 2016 cycle.

00:42:09.026 --> 00:42:12.596 align:start size:97% position:3%
And that's the one where
Megyn Kelly and Donald Trump,

00:42:12.596 --> 00:42:14.899
there's knockdown
drag out fight.

00:42:14.899 --> 00:42:16.233
And I was sitting at
home with Callista.

00:42:16.233 --> 00:42:17.868
We were watching the debate,

00:42:17.868 --> 00:42:21.906
but I was also watching
all the various online,

00:42:21.906 --> 00:42:23.541
like Time Magazine and Vogue

00:42:23.541 --> 00:42:25.509
and a whole bunch of, there
were all sorts of places

00:42:25.509 --> 00:42:28.579 align:start size:88% position:13%
you could go to register
who you thought won

00:42:28.579 --> 00:42:31.849
and about an hour and a
half after the debate.

00:42:31.849 --> 00:42:36.620
And I watched Luntz and what
I thought was one of the most

00:42:38.222 --> 00:42:41.392 align:start size:84% position:16%
inaccurate focus
groups he'd ever done.

00:42:41.392 --> 00:42:43.460
I like Frank a lot,
we are old friends.

00:42:43.460 --> 00:42:45.629
But what happened was,

00:42:45.629 --> 00:42:48.766
you'd have to be very careful
on things like focus groups

00:42:48.766 --> 00:42:51.802 align:start size:84% position:16%
because a group
mindset will set in.

00:42:51.802 --> 00:42:54.972 align:start size:94% position:6%
And if people conclude,
that he must have done bad

00:42:54.972 --> 00:42:55.973
because she said he did this,

00:42:55.973 --> 00:42:57.541
so he must have done bad.

00:42:57.541 --> 00:42:59.577
And I don't wanna be the guy
guys that said he did good.

00:42:59.577 --> 00:43:02.513
So what was happening
was all of the experts

00:43:02.513 --> 00:43:03.581
were trashing Trump.

00:43:04.782 --> 00:43:07.284
Luntz's focus group
was trashing Trump,

00:43:07.284 --> 00:43:12.189 align:start position:0%
but in a field of 16 candidates,
he was getting 60 to 70%

00:43:12.189 --> 00:43:13.991 align:start size:84% position:16%
of the vote in these
online surveys.

00:43:15.426 --> 00:43:17.261
And I remember turning to
Callista about midnight

00:43:17.261 --> 00:43:18.762
I guess, and saying,

00:43:18.762 --> 00:43:22.499
"As a historian, there's
an anomaly building here,

00:43:22.499 --> 00:43:26.203 align:start size:97% position:3%
"the gap between lead opinion
and the average American

00:43:26.203 --> 00:43:29.873
"is getting so big that we
should watch this carefully

00:43:29.873 --> 00:43:32.610
"because something's going
on, we don't understand."

00:43:32.610 --> 00:43:34.445 align:start size:84% position:16%
And that's really
how I approach that.

00:43:34.445 --> 00:43:37.314 align:start size:88% position:13%
I use a model of listen,
learn, help and lead.

00:43:37.314 --> 00:43:39.650 align:start size:84% position:16%
And I really find that
the world's so big

00:43:39.650 --> 00:43:41.552
and changes so constantly

00:43:41.552 --> 00:43:43.887
that I have to listen and
learn all the time and I...

00:43:43.887 --> 00:43:47.024
So I approach every
activity with a,

00:43:47.024 --> 00:43:49.460 align:start size:84% position:16%
"I wonder what I'll
learn from it."

00:43:49.460 --> 00:43:50.928
- Huh?

00:43:50.928 --> 00:43:52.429 align:start size:94% position:6%
You'll probably find this
interesting actually,

00:43:52.429 --> 00:43:54.131
when I had David Horowitz on
who I'm sure you either know,

00:43:54.131 --> 00:43:55.132
or certainly know this work.

00:43:55.132 --> 00:43:56.000
- Yeah, I know him.

00:43:56.000 --> 00:43:57.501
- I asked him,

00:43:57.501 --> 00:44:00.104
when did he realize that
Trump was the real deal?

00:44:00.104 --> 00:44:01.905
And he said, it was
the exact moment

00:44:01.905 --> 00:44:04.541
that Trump got into the
fight with Megyn Kelly

00:44:04.541 --> 00:44:07.111
because he realized nobody
else would have ever done it,

00:44:07.111 --> 00:44:08.278
and that was it.

00:44:08.278 --> 00:44:10.280
And he fully went there.

00:44:10.280 --> 00:44:12.516
Let's talk a little bit
about history though,

00:44:12.516 --> 00:44:14.084 align:start size:81% position:19%
because we seem
to be in this time

00:44:14.084 --> 00:44:16.954
where we're erasing history,

00:44:16.954 --> 00:44:21.058
is there something that
for my younger audience,

00:44:21.058 --> 00:44:22.926
some piece of American history

00:44:22.926 --> 00:44:25.863 align:start size:84% position:16%
that maybe isn't
the most known piece

00:44:25.863 --> 00:44:27.631
that you think people
should know more about

00:44:27.631 --> 00:44:30.300 align:start size:97% position:3%
that might help us get a
lens on what's going on now?

00:44:31.902 --> 00:44:33.370
- Sure.

00:44:33.370 --> 00:44:37.307 align:start size:94% position:6%
I wrote three novels about
the Revolutionary War

00:44:38.175 --> 00:44:41.211
and four novels about,

00:44:44.481 --> 00:44:46.717 align:start size:84% position:16%
no four novels
about the Civil War.

00:44:51.121 --> 00:44:54.892 align:start position:0%
I think the most amazing moment
in the history of America,

00:44:55.859 --> 00:44:59.596
is Christmas night, 1776.

00:45:00.998 --> 00:45:03.767 align:start position:0%
We did it in a book called "The
times That Try Men's Souls"

00:45:03.767 --> 00:45:08.672
which is the quote from
Paine's "The Crisis."

00:45:10.107 --> 00:45:14.812 align:start size:97% position:3%
Washington's army has shrunk
from 30,000 in September,

00:45:15.412 --> 00:45:16.613
to about 2,500.

00:45:18.115 --> 00:45:20.918
And they have a council of
war and Washington says,

00:45:22.086 --> 00:45:23.287
"Most of these guys,

00:45:25.556 --> 00:45:28.759
"enlistments end first or
second week of January.

00:45:30.160 --> 00:45:32.930 align:start size:97% position:3%
"And if we don't do something,
the army will disappear."

00:45:34.264 --> 00:45:36.800
And he says, "Now all
of us in this room,

00:45:36.800 --> 00:45:39.903
"have no risk because
if the army disappears,

00:45:39.903 --> 00:45:41.205
"we're all gonna be hung."

00:45:42.906 --> 00:45:45.008 align:start size:84% position:16%
So we can take really
big risks because

00:45:45.008 --> 00:45:45.943
(Newt laughs)

00:45:45.943 --> 00:45:47.411
our life's already forfeit.

00:45:47.411 --> 00:45:50.814
And so he came up with a
plan, which is complicated,

00:45:50.814 --> 00:45:52.282
really complicated,

00:45:52.282 --> 00:45:56.987
you cross a icy river in the
middle of a snow storm at night

00:45:57.855 --> 00:45:58.789
in three different places.

00:45:58.789 --> 00:46:00.224
He would lead the main elements.

00:46:01.925 --> 00:46:02.860
He had,

00:46:02.860 --> 00:46:03.794
(Newt clears throat)

00:46:03.794 --> 00:46:04.561
as a sign of great leadership.

00:46:06.029 --> 00:46:08.031
He had taken Thomas Paine
who had written an amazing

00:46:09.266 --> 00:46:10.968
pamphlet called "Common Sense"

00:46:10.968 --> 00:46:13.670
which explained the
Revolutionary War
to everyday people.

00:46:15.038 --> 00:46:17.908 align:start size:84% position:16%
And Paine had enlisted
as a rifleman.

00:46:17.908 --> 00:46:19.476 align:start size:84% position:16%
And on the way
through New Jersey,

00:46:19.476 --> 00:46:21.712 align:start size:84% position:16%
Washington took
him aside and said,

00:46:21.712 --> 00:46:24.248
"Go to Philadelphia
and write a pamphlet

00:46:24.248 --> 00:46:26.450
"that explains why...?

00:46:26.450 --> 00:46:27.785
"What do we do?

00:46:27.785 --> 00:46:29.653 align:start size:84% position:16%
"We all thought this
was gonna be easy

00:46:29.653 --> 00:46:30.988
"Well, it's gonna be hard.

00:46:30.988 --> 00:46:32.990
"And we need somebody
to explain it to us."

00:46:32.990 --> 00:46:34.725
So Paine literally had
gone to Philadelphia

00:46:34.725 --> 00:46:37.094
and wrote "The Crisis."

00:46:37.094 --> 00:46:41.398
Washington had his officers read

00:46:41.398 --> 00:46:43.567 align:start size:88% position:13%
from the opening
chapter of "The Crisis"

00:46:43.567 --> 00:46:47.704 align:start size:94% position:6%
as the men got in the
boats to cross the river.

00:46:47.704 --> 00:46:48.972
- [Rubin] Wow.

00:46:48.972 --> 00:46:50.474
- When they finished
crossing the river,

00:46:50.474 --> 00:46:54.111
they marched eight miles
in a snow storm at night.

00:46:54.111 --> 00:46:55.746
One third of them
did not have boots

00:46:55.746 --> 00:46:57.848
and had wrapped their
feet in burlap bags.

00:46:59.216 --> 00:47:00.717
The next morning they surprised

00:47:00.717 --> 00:47:04.922 align:start size:97% position:3%
the professional German
soldiers captured 800 of them

00:47:06.089 --> 00:47:07.858
and ran like crazy
and cross the river

00:47:07.858 --> 00:47:10.561
before the British
army could get there.

00:47:10.561 --> 00:47:13.597
The news of that victory,

00:47:13.597 --> 00:47:16.633 align:start size:88% position:13%
brought 15,000
volunteers in two weeks.

00:47:18.235 --> 00:47:20.704
It's the decisive
moment of the war.

00:47:20.704 --> 00:47:24.441
Now I cite that for younger
people because all of these

00:47:24.441 --> 00:47:28.979
totally uninformed idiots
who downplay Washington,

00:47:28.979 --> 00:47:31.582
have no idea what real life is.

00:47:31.582 --> 00:47:34.751 align:start position:0%
You know, real life is not being
coddled in some university,

00:47:34.751 --> 00:47:38.589
surrounded by people who make
sure you have a safe space.

00:47:38.589 --> 00:47:41.525
Real life is facing
gigantic challenges

00:47:41.525 --> 00:47:44.661
and finding the character and
the courage and the endurance

00:47:44.661 --> 00:47:47.197
to build something,
to do something real.

00:47:47.197 --> 00:47:51.034
And this country stands on
George Washington shoulders.

00:47:51.034 --> 00:47:53.604 align:start size:97% position:3%
We would not have won the
Revolutionary War without him.

00:47:53.604 --> 00:47:54.771
We might not have gotten

00:47:54.771 --> 00:47:56.807 align:start size:88% position:13%
the Constitutional
Convention without him.

00:47:56.807 --> 00:47:59.376
And he served as our first
president for eight years

00:47:59.376 --> 00:48:02.246 align:start size:94% position:6%
and established precedents
which to this day

00:48:02.246 --> 00:48:04.448
are the mark of freedom.

00:48:04.448 --> 00:48:07.284
And I would just
say it is tragic

00:48:07.284 --> 00:48:10.254
that we've allowed three
generations of brainwashing

00:48:10.254 --> 00:48:13.590 align:start size:97% position:3%
to destroy the understanding
of the real America

00:48:13.590 --> 00:48:15.325
for millions of young Americans.

00:48:17.561 --> 00:48:19.363 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Well, it's an
inspirational story,

00:48:19.363 --> 00:48:21.064
but I can't end with that.

00:48:21.064 --> 00:48:22.966
So we're gonna have to end.

00:48:22.966 --> 00:48:24.234
I can't end with that sort of,

00:48:24.234 --> 00:48:25.802
I can't end with
that sort of ending

00:48:25.802 --> 00:48:27.504
of what we've done to
the three generations.

00:48:27.504 --> 00:48:28.438
So let's clean it up.

00:48:28.438 --> 00:48:30.073
Let's clean it up in a way.

00:48:30.073 --> 00:48:32.376
I know you will do well,

00:48:32.376 --> 00:48:35.579 align:start size:97% position:3%
which is for all of the people
that are worried right now,

00:48:35.579 --> 00:48:37.080
they're worried
about their jobs.

00:48:37.080 --> 00:48:39.082
They're worried about the
political polarization.

00:48:39.082 --> 00:48:41.118 line:0%
They're worried about
lockdowns and COVID

00:48:41.118 --> 00:48:43.220 line:0%
and everything else.

00:48:43.220 --> 00:48:47.291 align:start position:0% line:0%
Can you paint a bit of a
future that they can be hopeful

00:48:47.291 --> 00:48:49.293 line:0%
for, that can get
us out of this?

00:48:50.260 --> 00:48:51.762
- Yeah.

00:48:51.762 --> 00:48:53.730 align:start size:97% position:3%
I mean, you see it beginning
to happen all around you.

00:48:53.730 --> 00:48:55.666
We're gonna have an
explosive increase

00:48:55.666 --> 00:48:57.334
in the ability to
educate people.

00:48:59.369 --> 00:49:02.839
The recent announcements by
Amazon and Google and others

00:49:02.839 --> 00:49:04.207
and Apple,

00:49:04.207 --> 00:49:07.144 align:start size:97% position:3%
we're gonna have remarkable
capacity to people

00:49:07.144 --> 00:49:08.845
go and get great job.

00:49:08.845 --> 00:49:10.314
We're gonna become once again,

00:49:10.314 --> 00:49:12.582 align:start size:94% position:6%
the number one industrial
country in the world,

00:49:12.582 --> 00:49:14.785 align:start size:97% position:3%
we're gonna remain the
number one producer of energy

00:49:14.785 --> 00:49:16.086
in the world.

00:49:16.086 --> 00:49:19.489
We're gonna get to
the moon by 2024.

00:49:19.489 --> 00:49:21.992
And we're gonna be
on Mars by 2033.

00:49:21.992 --> 00:49:23.460
Our children and grandchildren

00:49:23.460 --> 00:49:26.430 align:start size:94% position:6%
are going to be literally
mining asteroids

00:49:26.430 --> 00:49:31.068 align:start size:84% position:16%
and running commercial
hotels in space.

00:49:31.068 --> 00:49:34.304 align:start size:94% position:6%
We will have
breakthroughs in medicine.

00:49:34.304 --> 00:49:38.642
We're about to have, I think,
a cure for sickle cell anemia,

00:49:38.642 --> 00:49:41.645 align:start size:84% position:16%
which will be of
enormous importance to
the black community.

00:49:42.779 --> 00:49:47.250 align:start size:84% position:16%
When we get aging
declared as a disease,

00:49:47.250 --> 00:49:52.055
so that the NIH can actually
focus on lengthening lives.

00:49:52.055 --> 00:49:56.727 align:start size:97% position:3%
I think the odds are very
good that we are going to have

00:49:56.727 --> 00:50:01.732 align:start position:0%
something like the average
person living very healthfully,

00:50:02.899 --> 00:50:06.103
to 110 or 120, not
getting ancient,

00:50:06.103 --> 00:50:09.072 align:start size:94% position:6%
but literally being active
and having a full life.

00:50:09.072 --> 00:50:12.209 align:start size:97% position:3%
So I think the long run
trajectory of the human race

00:50:12.209 --> 00:50:13.043
is pretty good.

00:50:13.043 --> 00:50:13.877
Do we have problems?

00:50:13.877 --> 00:50:14.811
Sure.

00:50:14.811 --> 00:50:15.612
We've always had problems.

00:50:15.612 --> 00:50:17.114
It's the nature of life.

00:50:17.114 --> 00:50:21.752
But I think that young people
have every reason to believe

00:50:21.752 --> 00:50:23.286
that by the end of their lives,

00:50:23.286 --> 00:50:25.956 align:start size:97% position:3%
the world will be more
interesting, more exciting,

00:50:25.956 --> 00:50:27.357
more prosperous,

00:50:27.357 --> 00:50:29.860 align:start position:0%
and the opportunity for
adventure will be even greater.

00:50:29.860 --> 00:50:32.963
- I knew you could do it,
Mr. Speaker, how do we,

00:50:32.963 --> 00:50:34.398
where should I send people?

00:50:34.398 --> 00:50:36.933 align:start size:97% position:3%
You've got like 1,000 websites
and a million projects.

00:50:36.933 --> 00:50:38.502
Where can we send people to?

00:50:38.502 --> 00:50:41.171 align:start size:94% position:6%
You're not retiring
anytime soon I know that.

00:50:41.171 --> 00:50:43.373
- My newsletters, my
podcasts, my books,

00:50:43.373 --> 00:50:45.976
all those things
are Gingrich 360.

00:50:45.976 --> 00:50:48.078
They can also of course
get the books that either

00:50:48.078 --> 00:50:51.815
Barnes &amp; Noble, Amazon,
or any major bookstore.

00:50:51.815 --> 00:50:55.452 align:start size:97% position:3%
And I'm very proud of "Trump
and the American Future."

00:50:55.452 --> 00:50:58.555 align:start size:97% position:3%
'Cause I think it captures
some of this extraordinary gap

00:50:58.555 --> 00:51:00.791 align:start size:84% position:16%
between a bad
future with the left

00:51:00.791 --> 00:51:02.993
and an amazingly good future,

00:51:02.993 --> 00:51:04.861 align:start size:81% position:19%
if we win this
election this fall

